    "description": "Iran’s communication infrastructure connects mobile subscribers, fixed internet customers, domestic services, and international networks. Understanding it requires distinguishing infrastructure development from the availability of unrestricted, reliable communication. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Telecommunications", "Internet infrastructure", "Connectivity", "Network resilience", "Digital access"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T09:04:06Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T09:04:06Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Communication Infrastructure in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s mobile and fixed internet infrastructure, domestic connectivity, international access, and the limits of network measurements."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "infrastructure-and-access",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Infrastructure and usable access",
        "content": "Communication infrastructure is the foundation beneath online publishing, messaging, broadcasting distribution, and digital commerce. In Iran, its significance extends beyond the presence of a connection: a network can support domestic services while international destinations remain inaccessible. The Internet Society’s account of the 2026 shutdown also records periods when domestic services became unreachable, showing that local hosting alone did not guarantee continuity. [s2]\n\nThree questions therefore need separate answers. Is a connection physically available? Can it carry traffic reliably? Which services can that traffic reach? Treating these as interchangeable can make an expanding network appear more accessible than users actually experience. This distinction follows from the documented contrast between visible network routes, successful communication, and selectively restored services. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-the-branches",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading the infrastructure landscape",
        "content": "The branches in this guide separate mobile operators from internet providers and from the underlying networks and facilities. They also give the National Information Network its own page. That domestic architecture is important because infrastructure expansion and restrictions on international access have developed together; Freedom House’s assessment describes both investment in connectivity and the use of domestic infrastructure to restrict access. [s1]\n\nThese categories should be read together. A retail internet subscription describes the customer’s service, while a discussion of transmission or domestic infrastructure concerns the systems on which that service depends. A provider comparison cannot, by itself, establish whether customers retain access during a restriction affecting shared infrastructure. The documented national disruptions make that limitation especially consequential in Iran. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "measuring-connectivity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why a visible network may still be unusable",
        "content": "Network measurements describe different layers. Border Gateway Protocol, or BGP, distributes information about routes between networks. A route announcement indicates that an address range is advertised; it does not prove that users can exchange useful traffic with it. [s3]\n\nA research preprint revised in July 2026 compared routing information, internet scans, and active connection tests during Iranian shutdowns. Its abstract reports that routes could remain announced while traffic was discarded. It concludes that no single measurement reliably distinguishes working connectivity from its absence. Only the abstract was inspected for this page, so this account does not extend to unexamined technical findings in the paper. [s3]\n\nThis matters when interpreting headlines about restoration. A routing recovery, an increase in traffic, and the return of a particular application are different observations. None independently establishes universal access across providers, places, or services. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "measurement-diagram",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Different questions require different network evidence",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Assess communication availability\"] -->|\"Inspect routing\"| B[\"Are address ranges announced?\"]\n  A -->|\"Test connections\"| C[\"Can traffic reach destinations?\"]\n  A -->|\"Observe services\"| D[\"Can applications function?\"]\n  B -->|\"Contributes evidence\"| E[\"Compare findings across layers\"]\n  C -->|\"Contributes evidence\"| E\n  D -->|\"Contributes evidence\"| E\n```",
        "summary": "This is an editorial guide to interpreting evidence, not a map of Iranian network equipment. Its arrows describe assessment steps; a route announcement alone does not establish that applications work.",
        "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "disruption-and-continuity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Disruption and continuity",
        "content": "The Internet Society records a nationwide shutdown beginning in January 2026, an uneven easing of restrictions later that month, and another collapse in traffic after hostilities began in late February. Its incident record closes in May. These phases should not be flattened into a claim that every connection was continuously unavailable for the entire recorded interval. [s2]\n\nFilterwatch’s January investigation describes effects on domestic networks and telephone services as well as international access. It also reports selective restoration of approved websites and services. That account establishes a documented episode, rather than a guarantee about how future restrictions will operate. [s4]\n\nFor readers exploring Iranian media, the practical implication is that distribution depends on both network capacity and permission to communicate. Reliable assessment requires dated evidence about particular services and providers, alongside the physical infrastructure story. [s1][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s4"]
      }
    ],
