    "description": "Internet providers are the customer-facing part of a wider system of access networks, domestic services, and international connectivity. In Iran, provider choice must be understood alongside restrictions and disruptions that can affect multiple networks. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Internet providers", "Broadband", "Internet access", "Service availability", "Connectivity"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T09:04:06Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T09:04:06Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Internet Providers in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand internet provider choice in Iran, the limits of broadband statistics, and why domestic and international service availability differ."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "provider-and-access",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The provider and the wider network",
        "content": "An internet provider is the service relationship most visible to a customer, but the provider’s name does not describe every condition affecting access. Freedom House’s 2025 assessment identifies state control over infrastructure, obstacles to provider diversity, and content filtering as distinct constraints. A comparison of retail offers therefore cannot, by itself, explain the full experience of using the internet in Iran. [s1]\n\nThese constraints should also be distinguished from ordinary service performance. A connection may be slow, a destination may be blocked, or access may be interrupted more broadly. Each produces a different problem for someone trying to read news, upload a file, or communicate abroad. The documented pattern of selective restoration in 2026 shows why the availability of one service cannot establish the availability of others. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "domestic-and-international",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Domestic and international availability",
        "content": "The Internet Society’s incident record describes a nationwide shutdown beginning in January 2026, followed by partial and uneven restoration. It also records a renewed collapse in traffic after hostilities began in late February. Its description includes disruptions to domestic services, making it unsafe to assume that an Iranian website necessarily remained accessible when international access failed. [s2]\n\nFor provider research, this establishes a need for destination-specific evidence. A test of a domestic service and a test of an overseas service answer different questions. Neither should be substituted for a broad claim that a provider was fully online. The incident record is national in scope and does not supply a complete comparison of individual providers. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "broadband-statistics",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What broadband statistics can establish",
        "content": "The World Bank publishes a fixed broadband subscription indicator for Iran using the International Telecommunication Union’s World Telecommunication/ICT Indicators Database. The inspected page identifies the source and offers downloads. It is a national statistical series, rather than a directory of providers or evidence of the quality of a particular subscription. [s3]\n\nThe research interface did not expose a usable sequence of annual observations during inspection. This page therefore does not reproduce a trend chart or infer missing years. A defensible series requires the actual observations, their definitions, and any revisions or comparability notes. The existence of a chart interface is not enough to establish the values behind it. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "evidence-for-comparison",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Building a useful provider comparison",
        "content": "A dependable guide would pair verified provider documentation with dated evidence of service availability. It would keep advertised terms separate from measured outcomes and distinguish ordinary performance from restrictions affecting access. The 2025 assessment and the subsequent disruption record provide context for those distinctions, but they do not resolve which provider serves a particular address or offers the best service there. [s1][s2]\n\nThe present draft consequently provides an interpretive framework rather than a purchasing guide. Current provider identities, access technologies, local availability, and contractual terms remain to be verified before publication. This avoids turning incomplete national evidence into unsupported claims about individual services.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
